{"thought": "Now I see the bug clearly. In `FLAC__bitreader_read_rice_signed_block`, at the `process_tail` label:

```c
process_tail:
    do {
        // ... read unary MSBs and binary LSBs ...

        cwords = br->consumed_words;
        words = br->words;
        ucbits = FLAC__BITS_PER_WORD - br->consumed_bits;
        b = br->buffer[cwords] << br->consumed_bits;  // <-- BUG: cwords can be >= words here!
    } while(cwords >= words && val < end);
```

The issue is that after reading values in the `process_tail` loop, `cwords = br->consumed_words` and `words = br->words`. If `cwords >= words`, the loop continues. But then `b = br->buffer[cwords]` accesses `br->buffer[cwords]` where `cwords >= words`, which is out of bounds.

The fix would be to check `cwords >= words` before accessing `br->buffer[cwords]`.

Now I need to craft a FLAC file that triggers this. The key is to have a residual coding where:
1. The bitreader consumes all available words
2. The process_tail path is taken
3. After reading values, cwords >= words, so the loop continues
4. `br->buffer[cwords]` is accessed out of bounds

Let me look at the stream_decoder.c to understand how the residual is set up."}
